  "narrative": {
    "intro": "The Department of Energy obligated $48.4B on contracts in FY2026 to date. The agency recorded 4,092 awards with an average award size of $11.8M, and obligations were down 0.2% year over year. Facilities Support Services is the largest spending category at $24B, more than three times the next category, Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences at $7.5B. Multiple R&D subcategories and remediation and chemical manufacturing also appear in the top 10, including Remediation Services at $3.2B and Other Basic Inorganic Chemical Manufacturing at $2.9B. National laboratory management and site operations dominate the vendor list: TRIAD NATIONAL SECURITY, LLC received $5.2B, NATIONAL TECHNOLOGY & ENGINEERING SOLUTIONS OF SANDIA, LLC received $4.7B, and LAWRENCE LIVERMORE NATIONAL SECURITY, LLC received $3.4B. The FY26 to date totals are partial fiscal year figures. Historical context shows steady growth from FY21 through FY25, with FY25 at $48.5B and FY26 at $48.4B so far.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How has Energy's contract spending changed recently?",
        "body": "The Department of Energy obligated $48.4B in FY2026 to date, down 0.2% year over year. Obligations rose steadily from $37.7B in FY21 to $48.5B in FY25 before the slight decline into FY26. The FY26 total is a partial fiscal year figure."
      },
      {
        "heading": "What are Energy's biggest buying categories and who supplies them?",
        "body": "Facilities Support Services leads with $24B in obligations, followed by Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences at $7.5B. National laboratory and site contractors dominate the vendor list, led by TRIAD NATIONAL SECURITY, LLC at $5.2B and NATIONAL TECHNOLOGY & ENGINEERING SOLUTIONS OF SANDIA, LLC at $4.7B. Other notable categories include Remediation Services at $3.2B and Other Basic Inorganic Chemical Manufacturing at $2.9B."
      }
    ]
  },
  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "How much does Department of Energy spend on contracts?",
      "a": "The Department of Energy obligated $48.4B on contracts in FY2026 to date. The agency executed 4,092 awards with an average award size of $11.8M. Obligations were down 0.2% year over year, and the FY26 total is a partial fiscal year figure."
    },
    {
      "q": "What does Energy buy?",
      "a": "Energy buys a mix of support services, research and development, remediation, and chemical manufacturing. Facilities Support Services accounts for $24B, Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences accounts for $7.5B, Remediation Services accounts for $3.2B, and Other Basic Inorganic Chemical Manufacturing accounts for $2.9B. Several R&D subcategories and hazardous waste treatment also appear in the top 10."
    },
    {
      "q": "Who are Energy's largest contractors?",
      "a": "TRIAD NATIONAL SECURITY, LLC is the largest contractor at $5.2B, followed by NATIONAL TECHNOLOGY & ENGINEERING SOLUTIONS OF SANDIA, LLC at $4.7B and LAWRENCE LIVERMORE NATIONAL SECURITY, LLC at $3.4B. SAVANNAH RIVER NUCLEAR SOLUTIONS LLC is also at $3.4B, and other top vendors include CONSOLIDATED NUCLEAR SECURITY, LLC at $3.1B and UT-BATTELLE LLC at $2.6B."
